    "title": "Area food pantry steps up amid government shutdown - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Jake Cooperman ,\n1 days ago\nPITTSBURG, Kan. — As the government shutdown continues, a local food pantry is stepping up to help those in need in its community.\nThe Wesley House in Pittsburg has been working on bringing food to residents who no longer have access to their SNAP and WIC benefits.\nAccording to the ministry’s staff, a lot of the public relies on the pantry, even with federal food assistance in place.\nIt’s currently receiving sporadic donations of food and money, but, with the amount of people needing assistance, many times supplies dwindle by the end of each day.\nMatt O’Malley is the nonprofit’s executive director.\n“We have a very small staff and we couldn’t do all of this work without the volunteers. It’s really inspiring to see different churches, different businesses, different classrooms, different individuals all coming together to help Wesley House be a place that serves the most amount of food that we can serve to the most amount of people we can serve,” said Matt O’Malley, Wesley House Executive Director.\nAccording to O’Malley, the pantry is always in need of canned fruits, vegetables and meats.\nIt’s open for donations, weekdays, from 9:00 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KSNF/KODE | FourStatesHomepage.com.\n1",
